    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Hi all,<br><br></div>I ran across this figure and I thought it would be interesting as an interesting way to compare a multivariate data set. This is a plot describing some hockey statistics. However, I thought that it would interesting to apply this to the &quot;iris&quot; dataset. So for reference with a dummy set:<br><br><img style=\"margin-right:0px\" alt=\"https://s1.yimg.com/bt/api/res/1.2/3y_ZxwTaVtOCM3OaH3UYtQ--/YXBwaWQ9eW5ld3M7cT04NQ--/http://l.yimg.com/os/publish-images/sports/2015-04-20/949e1e50-e75e-11e4-88c1-f71b755bc7ab_graph1.png\" src=\"https://ci6.googleusercontent.com/proxy/Dk1IbCCZTYPJ7-HAHy3OBWPQL009sCC4o9ZPp52V_RihKWXb85OlFfXK3eyy9D7eOIQmLGe29CdUCYIx5biZ6DJRp3T4QO4Sh2S3bhFFu33jRCQVbKBE5PztYsrspP1VSYYY5k-TNxk7li7y-whfETq6cqLY9y7mu-y0iRo5yI46JBJ5SWh7-TCX5TXUZNvoE1I9OLB4wcvwamtb8MfKPc3W4GiwXqgqZeXSMjLw3B5L0nlcio-mfHz-BClEfWGWP3BtC00ko1uBg-LqqP0=s0-d-e1-ft#https://s1.yimg.com/bt/api/res/1.2/3y_ZxwTaVtOCM3OaH3UYtQ--/YXBwaWQ9eW5ld3M7cT04NQ--/http://l.yimg.com/os/publish-images/sports/2015-04-20/949e1e50-e75e-11e4-88c1-f71b755bc7ab_graph1.png\" height=\"284\" width=\"476\"><br><br><span style=\"border-collapse:separate;color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;font-size:13px;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;line-height:15px;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:pre-wrap;word-spacing:0px;background-color:rgb(225,226,229)\"><pre style=\"font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;font-size:10pt!important;outline-style:none;outline-width:initial;outline-color:initial;border-style:none;border-width:initial;border-color:initial;white-space:pre-wrap!important;word-break:break-all;margin:0px;line-height:1.2\"><span style=\"border-collapse:separate;color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;font-size:13px;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;line-height:15px;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:pre-wrap;word-spacing:0px;background-color:rgb(225,226,229)\"><pre style=\"font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;font-size:10pt!important;outline-style:none;outline-width:initial;outline-color:initial;border-style:none;border-width:initial;border-color:initial;white-space:pre-wrap!important;word-break:break-all;margin:0px;line-height:1.2\"><span style=\"white-space:pre;color:blue\"><br>&gt; </span><span style=\"color:blue\">iris[c(1,51,101),]\r\n</span>    Sepal.Length Sepal.Width Petal.Length Petal.Width    Species\r\n1            5.1         3.5          1.4         0.2     setosa\r\n51           7.0         3.2          4.7         1.4 versicolor\r\n101          6.3         3.3          6.0         2.5  virginica</pre></span></pre></span><div><div><br>So in this example you&#39;d divide the circle into four lines, then plot the suite of variables above with Species being the colour aesthetic connected by a line.<br><br></div><div>So my question is: Is this possible with ggplot2? Also but not specifically any input into this particular plot as a tool to visualize multivariate datasets. The thing that I like about this is that line pattern created should be a visual indication of similarity.<br><br></div><div>Thanks in advance.<br><br></div><div>Sam<br></div><div><br> <br>  <br><br></div></div></div>\r\n"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "AwtsIlcBNGoJ",
   "from": "Tom Philippi",
   "author": "4e979d95e92a2b9e",
   "date": "2015-04-20T21:10:30Z",
   "subject": "Re:",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">The trick is +coord_polar()<div>in your application you would want geom_line() + coord_polar()</div><div>the more usual wind rose uses geom_bar() + coord_polar()</div><div><br></div><div>Note that to do this, you need to reshape your dataset into long format: species, attribute name, value.  In your plot you are then mapping a factor (attribute name: sepal.length, sepal.width, etc.) into an order around the circle and connecting the dots between those categories, which would be perceived as areas by the audience, even though only the differences at the 4 data points are real.  The order of the categories will matter a great deal for whether different species appear different in your example.</div><div><br></div><div>In other words, it is easy to create this graph in ggplot2, but often (not always) it is an inappropriate and potentially misleading visualization of the data, especially for multiple variables that become factors mapped to the direction.</div><div><br></div><div>Tom 2</div><div class=\"gmail_extra\"><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\">On Mon, Apr 20, 2015 at 10:38 AM, Sam Albers <span dir=\"ltr\">&lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">tonights...@gmail.com</a>&gt;</span> wrote:<br></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Hi all,<br><br></div>I ran across this figure and I thought it would be interesting as an interesting way to compare a multivariate data set. This is a plot describing some hockey statistics. However, I thought that it would interesting to apply this to the &quot;iris&quot; dataset. So for reference with a dummy set:<br><br><img style=\"margin-right:0px\" alt=\"https://s1.yimg.com/bt/api/res/1.2/3y_ZxwTaVtOCM3OaH3UYtQ--/YXBwaWQ9eW5ld3M7cT04NQ--/http://l.yimg.com/os/publish-images/sports/2015-04-20/949e1e50-e75e-11e4-88c1-f71b755bc7ab_graph1.png\" height=\"284\" width=\"476\"><br><br><span style=\"border-collapse:separate;color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;font-size:13px;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;line-height:15px;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:pre-wrap;word-spacing:0px;background-color:rgb(225,226,229)\"><pre style=\"font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;font-size:10pt!important;outline-style:none;outline-width:initial;outline-color:initial;border-style:none;border-width:initial;border-color:initial;white-space:pre-wrap!important;word-break:break-all;margin:0px;line-height:1.2\"><span style=\"border-collapse:separate;color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;font-size:13px;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;line-height:15px;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:pre-wrap;word-spacing:0px;background-color:rgb(225,226,229)\"><pre style=\"font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;font-size:10pt!important;outline-style:none;outline-width:initial;outline-color:initial;border-style:none;border-width:initial;border-color:initial;white-space:pre-wrap!important;word-break:break-all;margin:0px;line-height:1.2\"><span style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;color:blue\"><br>&gt; </span><span style=\"color:blue\">iris[c(1,51,101),]\r\n</span>    Sepal.Length Sepal.Width Petal.Length Petal.Width    Species\r\n1            5.1         3.5          1.4         0.2     setosa\r\n51           7.0         3.2          4.7         1.4 versicolor\r\n101          6.3         3.3          6.0         2.5  virginica</pre></span></pre></span><div><div><br>So in this example you&#39;d divide the circle into four lines, then plot the suite of variables above with Species being the colour aesthetic connected by a line.<br><br></div><div>So my question is: Is this possible with ggplot2? Also but not specifically any input into this particular plot as a tool to visualize multivariate datasets. The thing that I like about this is that line pattern created should be a visual indication of similarity.<br><br></div><div>Thanks in advance.<br><br></div><div>Sam<span><font color=\"#888888\"><br></font></span></div><span><font color=\"#888888\"><div><br> <br>  <br><br></div></font></span></div></div></blockquote></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><span><font color=\"#888888\">\r\n\r\n<p></p>\r\n\r\n-- <br>\r\n-- <br>\r\nYou received this message because you are subscribed to the ggplot2 mailing list.<br>\r\nPlease provide a reproducible example: <a href=\"https://github.com/hadley/devtools/wiki/Reproducibility\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://github.com/hadley/devtools/wiki/Reproducibility&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792369227572000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3DNpyFZhhN-TCT1en6jJN2\">https://github.com/hadley/devtools/wiki/Reproducibility</a><br>\r\n <br>\r\nTo post: email <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">ggp...@googlegroups.com</a><br>\r\nTo unsubscribe: email <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">ggplot2+u...@googlegroups.com</a><br>\r\nMore options: <a href=\"http://groups.google.com/group/ggplot2\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://groups.google.com/group/ggplot2&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792369227572000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2KPYApKpn0ehaSoIJdJ3aq\">http://groups.google.com/group/ggplot2</a><br>\r\n<br>\r\n--- <br>\r\nYou received this message because you are subscribed to the Google Groups &quot;ggplot2&quot; group.<br>\r\nTo unsubscribe from this group and stop receiving emails from it, send an email to <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">ggplot2+u...@googlegroups.com</a>.<br>\r\nFor more options, visit <a href=\"https://groups.google.com/d/optout\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://groups.google.com/d/optout&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792369227572000&amp;usg=AOvVaw22fXXBnNlC6fBZFyvqVRkR\">https://groups.google.com/d/optout</a>.<br>\r\n</font></span></blockquote></div><br></div></div>\r\n"
